ion?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random” can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable “The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable “The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly” can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random”; “The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76b2b1b-7536-5b3c-a4c4-6f5e5b72e6ec" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19dfb7ab-264f-5b26-bc13-11fda1da144d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6a2885-5ee5-54dd-a91e-78ddd4a1fcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549